{"text": "Appendix 3\n\n+ CHEMICAL O83\nBASURE OF PERM:\n\nBEAD\n\u2018AVION AND CIGAR CHARACTER\n\nC. L. Tucker\n\nThe principle cigar tobaccos grown in the United States\n\nare Connecticutt Shade grown tobacco and Pennsylvania and\n\nWisconsin secd leaf tobaccos. The Connecticutt tobacco is\nused primarily for cigar wrapper and binder, whereas the\nPennsylvania and Wisconsin tobaccos are utilized as cigar\n\nfillers.\n\nese filler tobacces are grown and air cured by\n\nthe farmer. He then markets them in rectangular bales which\nare covered on four sides by Kraft paper. \u2018The buyer usually\nstores these bales by stacking them in large compact rectangular\n\nma!\n\nes, While in this type of storage, the interior bales undergo\n\na degree of fermentation as evidenced by a ris\n\ne in temperature\nPeriodically, these masses of bales axe rearranged so that the\n\nexterior bales are moved to the interior and\n\nBefore the tobacco is utilized in a product, the bales are\n\nopened, the tobacco fluffed up, the moisture increased, and\n\nadditional and more\n\nplaced in large bulks where it undergoes\nintensive fermentation.\nThe tobacco as received from the farmer in bales is\n\ngenerally referred to as cured tobacco. After storage in\n\nbales where it has undergone some fermentation, it is usually\n\nreferred to as \"naturally fermented\" tobacco. \u2018the final\nfermentation in large/loose leaf bulks at a high moisture\ncontent is commonly referred to as \u201cbulk fermented\" or \"force\n\nfermented\" tobacco.\n\np9T09088",
